By using the Peiffer lifting, we obtain a map 
$
({\rm id}_{g'_2}\#_1 h_1) \#_2 (h_2\#_1 {\rm id}_{g_1})
$ to $
(h_2\#_1 {\rm id}_{g'_1}) \#_2$ $ ({\rm id}_{g_2}\#_1 h_1).
$
\begin{equation}
  \begin{split} 
\label{pfmap}
\{h_2, \act{g_2}h_1 \}(\act{\partial(h_2)g_2}h_1\cdot h_2) &= \partial(\{h_2, \act{g_2}h_1 \})\cdot \act{\partial(h_2)g_2}h_1\cdot h_2\\
&=h_2\cdot \act{g_2}h_1\cdot h_2^{-1}\cdot \act{\partial(h_2)g_2}h_1^{-1}\cdot  \act{\partial(h_2)g_2} h_1\cdot h_2\\
&= h_2\cdot \act{g_2}h_1
 \end{split}
\end{equation}

Equation~(\ref{pfmap}) shows that $\{h_2, \act{g_2}h_1 \}$ defines a morphism from $({\rm id}_{g'_2}\#_1 h_1) \#_2 (h_2\#_1 {\rm id}_{g_1})$ to $(h_2\#_1 {\rm id}_{g'_1}) \#_2 ({\rm id}_{g_2}\#_1 h_1)$. If we assume $g_1=g_2=e_g$, equation~(\ref{pfmap}) can be written as $\{h_2, h_1 \}(\act{\partial(h_2)}h_1\cdot h_2) = h_2\cdot h_1$. The map $\{h_2, \act{g_2}h_1 \}$ is illustrated in Figure~\ref{d:PF_lift}.
